None — no security, correctness, or structural issues observed in this snippet beyond the performance bottleneck itself.

## Tests for: `parseAge` (src/age.js)

**Scenarios covered:**
- [x] Happy path: valid numeric string → integer
- [x] Boundary: 0, 150, 151 (max+1), -1 (min-1), very large numeric string
- [x] Error: empty string, whitespace-only, non-numeric string, `null`, `undefined`, object, array, boolean, `"Infinity"`, `NaN` — all should throw `RangeError` per the stated contract
- [x] Error (partial-parse garbage): `"12abc"`, `"150xyz"`, `"0x10"` — not valid numeric strings, should throw
- [x] Documented current behavior (not asserted as a required fix): truncating non-integer strings (`"10.9"`), accepting raw numbers (`42`)
- [x] Determinism: same input called twice yields same result
- [ ] External failure: N/A — pure function, no I/O, no dependencies to mock
- [ ] Concurrency/state: N/A beyond determinism — function is stateless

